# Member accounts and invites

For someone who needs ongoing access, invite them as a **member**. They get their
own login and you choose a **role** that decides what they can see and do. Unlike
a public or guest link, a member is a named person you can see in a list, change,
and remove.

## Inviting someone

From **Site settings > Sharing & Privacy**, create an invite. You pick the role
first, then Nocturne gives you a link to send them:

<Screenshot id="sharing-invite-card" />

The invite link opens a **join** page where they either sign in with an account
they already have or create one, and accepting adds them to your list of members.
From then on they have access until you change their role or remove them.

Two things to know about the link:

- **It expires.** By default an invite is good for 7 days. If it lapses before
  they use it, create another one; nothing is lost.
- **Treat it as private.** Until it's accepted, whoever opens the link can claim
  that role. Send it directly to the person, not to a group chat.

## Roles

Roles are the easiest way to set access. The common ones, from least to most
access:

| Role | What they can do |
|------|------------------|
| Viewer | Read-only: blood glucose and the whole Reports section. For a quick glance. |
| Clinician | Read-only access across all data, including therapy settings and reports. For a doctor reviewing your numbers. |
| Caretaker | Everything a Clinician sees. They can also log treatments, manage alerts, and set up data sources: connect, change, sync, and remove them. For someone who actively helps day to day. |
| Administrator | Full access to data and settings, including inviting and managing other people. For a co-parent or trusted manager. |
| Owner | You. Full control of the account; can't be removed. |

If a role is close but not quite right, you can give an individual member extra
**permissions** on top of it, without changing what the role means for everyone
else. Extra permissions only add access. To give one person less than a role
gives, give them a different role, or make a role of your own with only the
access they need.

You can also tick **Only last 24 hours** (when you invite them, or later) to
limit that member to recent data, the same way the public link's time window
works.

<Callout type="tip" title="When to use it: a teacher and a school nurse">

  At school, two people need access for the whole term, and they need different
  things. Invite the <strong>teacher as a Viewer</strong>; they just need to
  glance at the current glucose during class. Invite the
  <strong>school nurse as a Caretaker</strong>, so they can also log a treatment
  or manage an alert if they step in. A Caretaker can also set up data sources.
  If the nurse must not do that, give them a role of your own without data source
  setup, or remove data source setup from the Caretaker role, which changes it for
  every Caretaker. Each has their
  own login, you can see them in your member list, and when the year ends you
  remove them in one click.

</Callout>

## Access end date

A membership can end on a day that you choose. Use an end date for a person who needs access
for a limited time, such as a school term.

To set the day, use the **Access ends (optional)** field in one of these places:

- The invite form, before you create the invite.
- The approval of a membership request, before you approve it.
- A member row. Select **Edit**, pick the day, and select **Save changes**.

To remove an end date from a member, select **Edit** on the member row, then select
**Remove the end date** and **Save changes**.

The rules for an end date:

- **Access holds through the end of that day.** The end date is the last day of access. The day
  follows the time zone of the device you use when you pick it.
- **You cannot pick a day that has passed.** To end access now, remove the member.
- **Nothing is revoked.** When the day ends, the membership stops giving access, but the member
  keeps the same roles and permissions. If you set a later date, the member has the same access again.
- **An owner has no end date.** An owner's access does not end. Before you give a member the
  Owner role, remove their end date.
- **A person whose own access ends cannot give access that lasts longer.** If your access ends
  on a date, you cannot pick a later day. You cannot remove the end date of a member either.
  An invite or an approval with no day ends on the same day as your access. You also cannot
  change a member whose access lasts longer than yours, or change public sharing.

The member list shows **Until** and the date for a member whose access will end. After the date,
it shows **Ended** and the date. A person who opens an invite sees the day their access ends on
the join page.

## Membership requests

Inviting someone is one direction: you reach out and hand them a link.
**Membership requests** are the other direction: you let people **ask you** for
access, and nothing is granted until you approve it.

The toggle lives on **Site settings > Sharing & Privacy**. While it's on, your sign-in
page shows a **Request membership** link. Someone who lands on your instance types their name
and a short note saying who they are, presses **Send request**, and makes a passkey. It
**never grants access on its own**; it waits for approval.

You review requests **in place, on Site settings > Sharing & Privacy**, the same page
as the toggle, and you get a notification for each new one. Each one shows who asked, their
note, and when. Tick the roles they should have and approve to add them as a member, or deny
it. It's the same roles as an invite; the only difference is who started the conversation.

For what the person on the other side sees, and how to word the note, see
[Request membership](/docs/authentication/request-membership).

<Callout type="tip" title="When to use it: a relative who wants to follow along">

  A grandparent asks to keep an eye on your child's glucose. Rather than
  collecting their email and sending an invite, switch membership requests on and
  point them at your instance. They request access with a passkey, and you approve them as a
  <strong>Viewer</strong> when it suits you, or deny it if you change your mind.

</Callout>

## Removing a member

Change a member's role to a narrower one, or remove them entirely, from the member
list on **Site settings > Sharing & Privacy**. Removal takes effect immediately; their
own account still exists, it just no longer reaches your data.
